# Docker & Deployment

Target: Hostinger VPS via Docker. Nginx is the only public entry. Align with `Docs/[01] Main App/Tech Stack.md`. Exact Compose filenames/layout may evolve — do not invent microservices or bypass this model.

## Container responsibilities

| Service | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `nginx` | TLS termination, reverse proxy, static web assets (if served from container/host) |
| `api` (Express) | App API only — no public port except via Nginx |
| `postgres` | Source of truth data |
| `redis` | Cache / queue only |
| Other | Only when justified (e.g. workers); ask before adding |

- One concern per container. Do not put Postgres or Redis inside the API image.
- Mobile is not deployed as a Docker service on the VPS.

## Dockerfile rules

- Multi-stage builds for Node apps (build → lean runtime).
- Run as non-root; pin base image tags (avoid `:latest` in production).
- Copy dependency manifests first; leverage layer cache.
- Never bake secrets, `.env`, or private keys into images — inject at runtime.
- Production image: build artifacts + runtime deps only (no devDependencies, no source maps unless needed).
- `backend` Dockerfile lives with the backend; web static build is produced then served by Nginx (or a dedicated web image that only holds built assets).

## Docker Compose structure

- Prefer a root Compose file for the VPS stack: `nginx`, `api`, `postgres`, `redis` (+ optional workers).
- Use Compose profiles or a separate override for local vs production — do not commit production secrets.
- Services declare explicit `depends_on` where startup order matters; healthchecks for `postgres` / `api` / `redis`.
- Keep env via `env_file` / Compose `environment` referencing host/secrets — not hardcoded passwords in git.

## Nginx

- Sole publicly exposed HTTP/HTTPS endpoint.
- Proxy `/api/` (or `/api/v1/`) to the Express service on the internal Docker network.
- Serve the web SPA/static files; route unknown paths to `index.html` when using client-side routing.
- Do not expose Postgres, Redis, or Express ports to the public internet.

## Networking

- Put app services on a private Docker bridge network.
- Publish only Nginx ports (`80`/`443`) to the host.
- API reaches Postgres/Redis by Compose service DNS names — never via public IPs.
- Prefer internal URLs inside the network (`http://api:PORT`), not `localhost` from other containers.

## Volumes

- Persist Postgres data on a named volume (required).
- Persist Redis only if durability is required; otherwise ephemeral is acceptable for cache.
- Mount Nginx config/certs as needed; prefer managed cert volumes or host mounts — do not commit private keys.
- Do not bind-mount application source into production containers.

## Production configuration

- Restart policy: `unless-stopped` (or equivalent) for core services.
- Resource limits where the VPS is constrained; log to stdout/stderr for Docker logging.
- TLS at Nginx; HTTP→HTTPS redirect.
- Separate secrets from Compose committed to git (Hostinger env, Docker secrets, or untracked `.env`).
- Migrations run as an explicit release step (one-off container/job), not silently on every API start unless that pattern is already established.
- Ask before changing the public proxy layout, exposed ports, or adding new long-running services.
